 -0.01021575927734375, 0.0195770263671875, 0.0151824951171875, 0.00237274169921875, 0.025665283203125, -0.013641357421875, 0.00316619873046875, -0.018402099609375, 0.0107574462890625, 0.03778076171875, 0.040283203125, -0.0032711029052734375, 0.007110595703125, -0.026031494140625, -0.0251007080078125, 0.023590087890625, -0.01641845703125, 0.00965118408203125, -0.0006761550903320312, -0.044464111328125, -0.020355224609375, 0.01165771484375, -0.004329681396484375, 0.017852783203125, 0.036773681640625, -0.0214996337890625, -0.00887298583984375, -0.00864410400390625, 0.005962371826171875, -0.005054473876953125, -0.0030689239501953125, 0.007083892822265625, -0.0201263427734375, -0.0170745849609375, 0.00997161865234375, -0.0244293212890625, -0.01111602783203125, -0.006717681884765625, 0.00689697265625, 0.01558685302734375, -0.0157623291015625, -0.0003104209899902344, -0.002841949462890625, 0.0156402587890625, 0.00884246826171875, -0.0408935546875, -0.021270751953125, -0.005329132080078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) raises important questions about governance and control: Who sets direction, and when? How much autonomy should AI systems receive\u2014and through which mechanisms do humans provide guidance? This article investigates when, how, and through what mechanisms humans guide AI work, and how these patterns change depending on modes of human-AI interaction.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\nWe distinguish between two modes of delegation, each defined by the timing and extent of human input. Table 1 below summarizes definitions, boundary rules, and examples. (A full decision tree appears in Supplementary Figure 1, described in the data repository linked at [URL].)\n\n| Mode                 | Core Definition                                                         | Boundary Rule                                                                              | Example(s)                                                                                               |\n|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Specified delegation | Human sets explicit goals or constraints up front; minimal feedback after initial instructions, before output is finalized. | If substantial feedback or adjustment occurs after generation begins, not considered purely specified. | User submits a prompt with required word limits and key topics; accepts first AI summary without revision. Iteratively tweaking a prompt before submitting for generation still counts as specified delegation. |\n| Iterative coproduction | Human collaborates interactively, providing feedback or adjustments after initial AI output. | If user reviews and revises outputs interactively during and after creation, it is iterative coproduction regardless of initial instructions. | User reviews AI draft, requests changes to specific sections, and repeats this process until satisfied.   |\n\nIn borderline cases (e.g., a user repeatedly edits their prompt before the AI generates any content), all human input before generation is classified as specified delegation. Only feedback after intermediate or final output constitutes iterative coproduction.\n\n*Delegation* refers to assigning cognitive steps from human to machine. *Prior human direction* refers to human intent shaping the task before AI execution.\n\n**Research Questions**\n\nThis study asks:  \n1. At which stages are humans most influential in shaping AI output during task delegation?  \n2. How do delegation patterns differ between structured (API-based) and interactive (chat-based) environments?\n\n**Methodology**\n\nTo improve transparency and flow, we divide methodology into three stages, briefly summarizing the rationale for each.\n\n1. **Dataset and Preprocessing**\n\nWe use the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI), a dataset of over 2 million anonymized records of human-AI task interactions from April\u2013May 2026. AEI records include task types, interaction processes, context, and relevant user characteristics. The dataset is concentrated on U.S.-based, English-speaking knowledge workers (over 80% of records), predominantly from administration, technology, communications, and business sectors; creative, manufacturing, and non-English-speaking sectors are underrepresented\u2014see Supplementary Table S1 for sample breakdowns.\n\nParticipation is voluntary, so occupational, geographic, and language biases are present. Preprocessing consisted of removing incomplete records and standardizing task types (using O*NET task codes, \u201clevel-0\u201d denoting the most detailed task). Analyses are conducted at the node-month-task level to capture fine-grained patterns.\n\n2. **Operationalization of Work Orientation**\n\nWe estimate the effect of increased professional use intent via a statistical counterfactual. Within qualifying records (with both work and personal usage details), we predict delegation outcomes as if the share of work-related intent rose by 10 percentage points, holding other variables constant. This is implemented using linear regression models with node and month fixed effects; the outcome is a normalized delegation score (mean=0, SD=1). Covariates include task type (O*NET level-0), user sector, and monthly usage volume; only node-month pairs present in both API and Claude.ai settings are included (n=1,411), ensuring fair comparisons (common support). Sensitivity checks with alternative specifications (see Supplementary Methods) confirm robustness.\n\nWe stress that this approach estimates marginal, not causal, effects\u2014actual interventions may produce different results, and unmeasured confounders may influence findings.\n\n3. **Statistical Analysis**\n\nOur primary comparison is between two interaction modes:\n- **1P API:** Direct access to Anthropic\u2019s API, primarily for technical or structured workflows.\n- **Claude.ai:** Web-based interfaces (e.g., Chat, Cowork), favoring interactive, conversational exchanges.\n\nLinear regression (with node and month fixed effects) estimates the association between a modeled 10% increase in work orientation and delegation mode prevalence, controlling for user and task characteristics. Robust standard errors are clustered by node. Key outcome: normalized delegation score by delegation type.\n\nFor interpretability, a one-point shift on the normalized delegation score is equivalent to moving from median (50th percentile) to ~90th percentile in frequency of up-front requirement setting (e.g., from specifying detailed instructions in ~40% of tasks to ~85%).\n\n**Findings and Interpretation**\n\n- When AI use is modeled as 10 percentage points more work-oriented, **specified delegation** increases by 2.76 standard deviations (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) in API mode and 1.45 (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) in Claude.ai. In practical terms, users in the 90th percentile for detailed up-front requirements become typical among work-oriented API users.\n- For **iterative coproduction,** scores decrease modestly in API mode (-0.30 SD) but increase in Claude.ai (+0.15 SD), yielding a clear, statistically significant gap (difference: 0.45 SD; 95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]); see Supplementary Table S2 for subgroup analyses by sector and task category.\n- Variability is notable: administrative and business sectors exhibit greater increases in specified delegation, while creative and communication tasks show higher iterative coproduction in Claude.ai mode.\n\nThus, for professionalized tasks, APIs promote up-front, precise specification\u2014delegation becomes more rigid and autonomous. In contrast, chat-based interfaces encourage ongoing oversight and collaboration, especially in creative or ambiguous tasks.\n\n**Limitations and Boundary Conditions**\n\nOur findings should be interpreted within several constraints:\n\n- **Sample Representativeness:** The AEI sample is heavily weighted toward U.S.-based office professionals; other sectors, languages, and regions are underrepresented.\n- **Causal Inference:** Statistical modeling simulates, but does not experimentally manipulate, work orientation; results may reflect unobserved differences in user skill, task complexity, or organizational practice. Robustness checks (alternative controls, subsample analyses) confirm main findings but cannot address all omitted variables.\n- **Effect Size Generalization:** Marginal estimates may not extrapolate to large or non-linear changes in intent, nor to new user groups or tasks with fundamentally different delegation opportunities.\n- **Interface-Selection Confounds:** Developers who prefer APIs may already be predisposed to detailed specification; organizational standards may codify delegation by interface.\n- **Applicability Scope:** Findings are most relevant to generative AI in structured, cognitive-intensive, non-embodied settings. Extrapolation to robotics, high-risk fields, or wholly creative tasks should be cautious.\n\nFuture work can mitigate these by recruiting more diverse samples, employing experimental designs, and directly measuring outcome variables (creativity, error rates, satisfaction, safety).\n\n**Implications and Future Directions**\n\nActionable recommendations for organizations and designers include:\n- For routine or risk-sensitive knowledge tasks, mandate submission templates requiring explicit, detailed up-front instructions\u2014especially for API-based systems.\n- For creative or open-ended work, integrate real-time feedback prompts and iterative checkpoints within chat or interactive UIs.\n- Implement role- or context-based UI defaults (e.g., \u201capproval required before output execution\u201d for high-stakes domains; \u201crapid prototyping mode\u201d for ideation tasks).\n- Establish policy frameworks that allocate human oversight at critical junctures, leveraging delegation mode as a design parameter.\n\n**Future research** should:\n- Use randomized experiments to test how interface structures (API vs. chat) causally affect delegation behavior.\n- Track user patterns longitudinally to evaluate the long-term effects of interface modality and organizational policy.\n- Expand sampling to cover non-English speakers, other sectors, and non-cognitive task domains.\n- Link delegation patterns to outcomes\u2014creativity, satisfaction, accuracy, and safety.\n\nSupplementary materials\u2014including the delegation mode decision tree, O*NET mapping crosswalks, robustness checks, and subgroup findings\u2014are accessible at [project repository URL].\n\nBy clarifying *when* and *how* human guidance shapes AI-mediated work, our analysis reframes governance not as a question of simple automation, but of timing, interface, and the levers by which human intent persists throughout distinct workflow settings."}}